Dear all,

Firstly thanks for your great work on INLA! I just started using it for my analysis and it is just what I needed and I enjoy it a lot.

My question is: How can I implement random slopes in a spde-model in Inla?.

In my analysis I have fixed effects, random effects (for Species) and a SPDE spatial random effect. (I copied Species to be able to use it twice).So my model formula would look something like this:

f <- y ~ -1 + Intercept + Temperature + other_Covariates +
        f(Species, model = "iid") +                # Random Intercept for Species
        f(Species2, Temperature, model = "iid") +  # Random Slope for Species
        f(w, model = spde)                                                     # Spatial random effect

What I haven’t been able to figure out is how to adapt the stack. The version with just the random intercepts for Species works fine and the stack for that looks like this:

Stack <- inla.stack(
            tag = "Fit",
            data = list(y = D$DOY),            # Response variable
          A = list(1, 1, A_spde, 1),   # A_spde = from inla.spde.make.A()
          effects = list(
              Intercept = rep(1, N),
              X = X,                   # Matrix with covariates
              w = w,                   # Spatial component from spde
              Species = D.ce$Species   # Random intercept for Species
              ))

I guess I have to add and adjust something here dealing with the „f(Species2, Temperature)“ part, but I don’t know how.
The model I used so far would be:

Model <- inla(f,
                   family = "gaussian",
                   data=inla.stack.data(Stack),
                   control.compute = list(dic = TRUE),
                   control.predictor = list(A = inla.stack.A(Stack)))

I would be immensely grateful for any help solving this! And I apologize in case this is already covered somewhere and I overlooked it.

Best,
Franziska

