Hi all,

I need to fit a geometric model to some data. It's not in the list of likelihoods in R-INLA. Indeed, the nbinomial likelihood with a dispersion parameter equal to 1 could be worked. How can I fix this parameter in a nbinomial model in R-INLA package?

I appreciate any help you can provide.
Hossein

From u3eab71733027a24a at groups.google.com  Sun Sep 27 21:23:51 2020
From: u3eab71733027a24a at groups.google.com (Helpdesk)
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2020 21:23:51 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] NegBinomial model with a known value for dispersion parameter
Message-ID: <8jqPckeiG94.uVqgD_NTAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <8jqPckeiG94.iRuojM5EAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <8jqPckeiG94.iRuojM5EAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/8jqPckeiG94/m/uVqgD_NTAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

with a likelihood like

	(1-p)^(k-1) * p

you can code this two likelihoods, one Binomial with k-1 trials and 0
successes, and then one binomial with 1 trial and 1 success.

only the marginal likelihood will be different, and you need to subtract
\sum\log((k-1)!), I *think*.

Otherwise, I can always add the 'plain' version in a few days.

Best
H

