Hello again Finn,

I am not sure about what you mean by the size of the graph. The original map has 246 polygons and so n_locs=246. I have tried the following:

n_locs  <- max(bru_vars$ID.area)
n_count <- max(bru_vars$ID.country)
n_month <- max(bru_vars$ID.month)
n_years <- 100 #

f1 <- total ~
  struct(ID.area, model='besag', graph=H,
         scale.model=TRUE, n=n_locs) +
  unstruct(map=ID.area1, model="iid", n=n_locs) +

  cnts(map=ID.country, model="iid", n=n_count) +
  rseas(map=ID.month, model="iid", n=n_month) +
  rtrend(map=ID.year, model="iid", n=n_years) +
  tas02 + pre02 + hur02 + population_density

mod1 <- bru(as.formula(f1),
            family="nbinomial",
            data=bru_vars,
            options=list(offset(log(bru_vars$population)),
                         control.inla=list(strategy="gaussian",

                                           int.strategy="eb"),
                         verbose=TRUE))

And all I got was the following error message:

Error in (function (data, model, stackmaker, n = 10, result = NULL, family,  :

  INLA returned message: subscript out of bounds
In addition: Warning message:
In parse(text = s, keep.source = FALSE)[[1L]] : subscript out of bounds

I am really keen on making this work as it would save me lots of computational time.

I look forward to hearing from you

Best wishes,
Felipe

